ons; but compiler may still fuse if allowed. We could use intrinsics like vmulq_f32 then vaddq_f32; on ARM64 with -ffp-contract=fast (default for GCC at -O3? Actually default is fast for non-strict FP? For C, GCC defaults to -ffp-contract=fast unless -std=c99? I think default is fast. But the reference likely compiled similarly? The official sequential code probably also uses same compiler flags; if we use vectorized operations that produce different rounding due to FMA contraction, output may differ.\n\nGiven many previous attempts have achieved ~0.155s with OpenMP and SoA etc., maybe they already used SIMD successfully while preserving exactness? Let's examine history: best score 0.8117 at mean 0.1557 from experiment 6be2b0550d04446e (role B). That likely involved some optimization. Our current source is the baseline? Actually current_source_paths shows src/nbody.c which appears to be original AoS with OpenMP parallel for schedule(static) and position update also parallel. This might be from parent 5308fd77f50646d0 (OpenMP parallelization). The selected_parent is that experiment, mean 0.1866. So we need improve beyond.\n\nWe can propose a new transformation: use manual NEON intrinsics to vectorize across i while preserving per-i summation order. But careful with alignment and memory layout. Current AoS struct Body has six floats; for each j we access p[j].x etc., which are strided by 6 floats (24 bytes). For SIMD across i, we need gather loads of x,y,z for four consecutive i's? That would be inefficient due to stride. Better to convert to SoA layout: separate arrays px,py,pz,vx,vy,vz. Previous experiments already did SoA and got ~0.176s (7f7d46710feb4b8c). Then with static scheduling etc got 0.160s. The best 0.1557 likely used something else.\n\nWe need propose one candidate. Since role B focuses on legal SIMD/vectorization and loop dependencies, we can try to manually vectorize the inner j loop across i using SoA arrays and NEON intrinsics, processing four bodies