scalar per i, so no cross-iteration dependency; but there is a reduction (fx += ...). Compiler can vectorize with reductions if allowed to reassociate. But we want exact order, so compiler may not vectorize due to strict FP unless -ffast-math. So manual intrinsics guarantee SIMD while preserving order.\n\nLet's write code using ARM NEON intrinsics. Need include <arm_neon.h>. We'll process 4 i's at a time: for (i = 0; i + 3 < n; i += 4) { float32x4_t fx, fy, fz; initialize to zero; loop j=0..n-1: load p[j] as scalar floats pxj, pyj, pzj; compute dx vector = vsubq_f32(vdupq_n_f32(pxj), x_i_vec) where x_i_vec is loaded from px[i..i+3]; similarly dy, dz. Then sqrd = vmulq(dx,dx)+vmulq(dy,dy)+vmulq(dz,dz)+vdupq(SOFTENING); inv_dist = vrecpe? Actually we need 1/sqrt(sqrd). Use vsqrtq_f32 then vdivq_f32(vdupq_n_f32(1.0f), sqrt_vec) or use vrsqrteq + Newton-Raphson for full precision? To match scalar exactly, we should compute inv_dist = 1.0f / sqrt(sqrd). On ARM64, there is a vector instruction FSQRT and FDIV; using intrinsics `vsqrtq_f32` and then `vdivq_f32(vdupq_n_f32(1.0f), vsqrt)` should produce same results as scalar if the compiler uses IEEE operations. However, scalar code might use library sqrt which is correctly rounded; vector FSQRT also correctly rounded? ARM NEON has VSQRT.F32 that is IEEE compliant (I think). Division FDIV is also IEEE. So bit-exact.\n\nThen inv_dist3 = vmulq(vmulq(inv,inv),inv); then fx += dx * inv_dist3 etc.\n\nAfter j loop, update velocities: vx[i..i+3] += dt*fx; etc.\n\nTail for remaining i's use scalar code identical to original.\n\nBut we must ensure that the order of operations per lane is exactly same as scalar. The vector operations compute each lane independently with same sequence: dx = pxj - x_i, dy..., sqrd = dx*dx + dy*dy + dz*dz + SOFTENING; inv_dist = 1/sqrt(sqrd); inv_dist3 = inv_dist * inv_dist * inv_dist; fx += dx * inv_dist3. This matches scalar exactly if each operation is IEEE and no FMA contraction. We can disable FP contraction by using `#pragma STDC FP_CONTRACT OFF` at file scope? In C, this pragma should be placed before any code. GCC supports it. Let's add that to ensure no fusion.\n\nAlso need to handle alignment: use posix_memalign or aligned_alloc for 16-byte (or 64) alignment. But NEON loads can be unaligned; we can use vld1q_f32 which works with any alignment? On ARM, unaligned vector load is allowed but may have performance penalty. We'll align to 16 bytes at least.\n\nWe also need to modify read/write functions: read into temporary Body array then split into SoA arrays; after simulation, write by interleaving from SoA back to a temporary Body buffer or directly fwrite with loop? The output must be exactly same binary format: int nbodies followed by six floats per body in order x,y,z,vx,vy,vz. We can allocate an array of Body and fill it then fwrite once.\n\nBut we need to preserve the exact input reading behavior: read_dataset currently reads nbodies as int (4 bytes) then bodies. We'll keep that but store into SoA arrays.\n\nWe also need to update position loop similarly using SIMD? Could use scalar or vector; not critical.\n\nLet's design code carefully.\n\nPotential issue: The original body_force uses `#pragma omp parallel for schedule(static)` on i. If we process groups of 4, the OpenMP loop should iterate over blocks of 4 to avoid splitting a group across threads? Actually each thread handles some i values; if we use #pragma omp parallel for with default chunking (contiguous), and inside we process 4 at a time, it's fine as long as n is multiple of 4 or tail handled. But OpenMP will assign contiguous ranges of i to threads; our inner loop processes groups within that range. That preserves per-i order.\n\nAlternatively, we can parallelize over j? No.\n\nLet's write the code.\n\nWe'll include <arm_neon.h> and use intrinsics. Also add `#pragma STDC FP_CONTRACT OFF` at top after includes.\n\nBut note: The compiler may still contract if pragma not honored; but GCC honors